\subsubsection{Sensitivity and degradation-case study}
\label{sec:ou-robustness}

The confirmatory comparison above is complemented by a declared
OU--III robustness study using the same ten paired wave-realization, IMU-noise, and
initialization seed triplets.  At the nominal $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$ sea, the
frozen operating point is perturbed by the multipliers $0.5$, $0.75$, $1.0$,
$1.25$, and $1.5$ along five directions.  Three are one-factor-at-a-time:
the OU correlation time $\tau$, the stationary acceleration scale
$\sigma_{aw}$, and the pseudo-measurement standard deviation $r_S$, each
varied with the other two held at the nominal point.  Because
$\mat{R}_S=\diag(r_S^2)$, multiplying $r_S$ by $c$ multiplies the
corresponding covariance by $c^2$.

The remaining two directions are \emph{coupled}, because the one-factor sweeps
answer a narrower question than they appear to.  The deployed law ties the
regularization scale to the other two parameters through
$r_S=\clip(0.35\,\sigma_{aw}\tau^{3},\,0.4,\,400)$, so freezing $r_S$ while
sweeping $\sigma_{aw}$ or $\tau$ measures only the \emph{direct} effect of the
OU process covariance and never reproduces a state the online tuner can
occupy.  The coupled sweeps therefore move $\sigma_{aw}\to c\,\sigma_{aw}$
with $r_S\to c\,r_S$, and $\tau\to c\,\tau$ with $r_S\to c^{3}r_S$.  All five
directions remain local perturbations about one point; none is a global
interaction study.

Two deliberately difficult inputs delimit the demonstrated operating envelope.
The first scales the smallest JONSWAP record from $H_s=\SI{0.27}{m}$ to
$H_s=\SI{0.05}{m}$ without changing its spectral shape, placing wave motion
closer to the fixed IMU-noise floor.  The second compresses the controlled
$H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$, $T_p=\SI{5.7}{s}$ to $H_s=\SI{4.0}{m}$,
$T_p=\SI{11.4}{s}$ ramp from \SI{360}{s} to \SI{30}{s}, while keeping the
transition midpoint, endpoint, long scoring window, and paired realizations
fixed.  These are degradation probes rather than additional population claims
about arbitrary ocean spectra.
Both ramps use a $C^2$ quintic weight, and their stored velocity and
acceleration include the exact first- and second-derivative blend terms.

