All five sweep directions share the $\times1.0$ reference and reproduce a mean
normalized vertical error of
\OURobustnessNominalMean\% of $H_s$ there.  Across the declared multiplier
range, the largest mean is \OURobustnessWorstMean\% for
the \OURobustnessWorstParameter{} direction at
$\times\OURobustnessWorstScale$; its paired difference from the nominal point
is \OURobustnessWorstDifference{} percentage points (bootstrap 95\% interval
$[\OURobustnessWorstDifferenceLow,\OURobustnessWorstDifferenceHigh]$).
Table~\ref{tab:ou_robustness_sensitivity} and
Fig.~\ref{fig:ou_robustness_sensitivity} report the complete sweep, so
robustness is assessed from the shape and intervals rather than from the most
favorable setting.

The sweep also exposes competing objectives.  Halving $r_S$ changes the 3D
displacement RMS by \OURobustnessRSHalfThreeDDifference{} m
($[\OURobustnessRSHalfThreeDLow,\OURobustnessRSHalfThreeDHigh]$) even though
it lowers the vertical-only score.  Halving $\sigma_{aw}$ changes pitch RMS by
\OURobustnessSigmaHalfPitchDifference{} degrees
($[\OURobustnessSigmaHalfPitchLow,\OURobustnessSigmaHalfPitchHigh]$).
Consequently, no sweep point is adopted as a replacement tuning: choosing one
from reference errors would violate the frozen, sensor-only tuning protocol and
would optimize one metric at the expense of another.

\paragraph{What the flat $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ columns do and do not mean}
With their companions frozen, the $\tau$ and $\sigma_{aw}$ columns move mean
vertical error by only \OURobustnessTauSpan{} and \OURobustnessSigmaSpan{}
points of $H_s$ across the full $\times0.5$ to $\times1.5$ range, whereas the
$r_S$ column moves it by \OURobustnessRSSpan{} points and the coupled sweeps
by \OURobustnessCoupledSigmaSpan{} and \OURobustnessCoupledTauSpan{} points.
The flatness is not a code path that fails to apply the parameter: $\sigma_{aw}^2$
enters the discrete OU process covariance, the raw records confirm that the
applied values match the requested values, and halving $\sigma_{aw}$ visibly
degrades pitch, as reported above.  It is a structural property of the
measurement geometry.  The accelerometer supplies a direct \SI{200}{Hz}
observation of the latent $\vct{a}_w$ with measurement noise several orders of
magnitude below the stationary OU variance, so the corresponding gain is
already near saturation and rescaling the prior by a factor of three barely
changes the latent-acceleration estimate that the kinematic chain integrates.
Over the same interval $\Delta t/\tau\lesssim10^{-2}$, so the OU mean reversion
between updates is negligible.  Displacement error is instead set by the
integral pseudo-measurement scale $r_S$, which is the only one of the three
that is not overwhelmed by a direct high-rate observation.  Attitude behaves
differently because $\mat{P}_{a_w a_w}$ enters the accelerometer innovation
covariance and therefore governs how the residual is apportioned between tilt
error and wave acceleration.

The correct statement is thus narrow: with $\tau$ and $r_S$ frozen at the
nominal point, displacement is locally insensitive to the direct OU
process-covariance effect of $\sigma_{aw}$, while attitude is not.  It is not
that the filter is insensitive to its OU tuning; the coupled columns, which
move $r_S$ the way the deployed law does, show the sensitivity the online
tuner actually experiences.

At $H_s=\SI{0.05}{m}$, the mean absolute vertical RMS error is
\SI{\OURobustnessLowStressAbsolute}{m}, corresponding to
\OURobustnessLowStressMean\% of $H_s$, compared with
\OURobustnessLowReferenceMean\% at $H_s=\SI{0.27}{m}$.  The paired normalized
difference is \OURobustnessLowDifference{} points
($[\OURobustnessLowDifferenceLow,\OURobustnessLowDifferenceHigh]$), explicitly
showing the limit imposed by a nearly fixed absolute sensor-error floor.

Compressing the transition changes the Adaptive mean from
\OURobustnessControlledAdaptiveMean\% to
\OURobustnessRapidAdaptiveMean\% of final $H_s$; the rapid-minus-controlled
paired difference is \OURobustnessRapidDifference{} points
($[\OURobustnessRapidDifferenceLow,\OURobustnessRapidDifferenceHigh]$).
Within the rapid case, Adaptive-minus-FixedNominal is
\OURobustnessRapidAdaptationDifference{} points
($[\OURobustnessRapidAdaptationLow,\OURobustnessRapidAdaptationHigh]$).
Positive differences denote higher Adaptive error.

That degradation must not be read as a pure measure of adaptation rate.
Compressing the ramp from \SI{360}{s} to \SI{30}{s} about a fixed midpoint
changes the composition of the fixed \SI{900}{s} window as well as its speed:
the rapid case contains far more pure-endpoint data and almost no crossfade,
whereas the controlled case spends \SI{360}{s} in the blend.  The two cases
therefore differ in what is scored, not only in how fast the sea moves, and the
reported difference confounds the two.  What these results establish is that
the filter degrades by a bounded, measured amount over a much faster specified
excursion; isolating the response limit of the adaptation smoothing would
require holding the scored sea-state composition fixed while varying only the
ramp rate, which the crossfade construction cannot do.
